1
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:18,488
The book of Acts says that people from Parthia, Media and Mesopotamia were among those who heard the apostles at Pentecost. Later tradition says this planted the first seed of Christianity in Persia.

2
00:00:19,138 --> 00:00:26,726
The Sasanian Empire ruled Persia from two twenty-four until the Arab conquest, and Christian communities grew within it.

3
00:00:27,376 --> 00:00:35,129
In four ten, about forty bishops met at Seleucia-Ctesiphon, and accepted the decisions of the Council of Nicaea.

4
00:00:35,779 --> 00:00:54,935
The Persian church also suffered. In the fourth century, the church leader Simeon bar Sabbae was executed after refusing to collect a double tax, and many Christians were remembered as martyrs. Historians still debate how many, and why.

5
00:00:55,585 --> 00:01:11,131
In six thirty-five, a Church of the East missionary named Olopun reached the Chinese capital. In seven eighty-one, a stone monument, the Xi'an Stele, was raised to tell the story.

6
00:01:11,781 --> 00:01:18,111
Along the Silk Road, Church of the East missionaries and traders carried the faith deep into Central Asia.

7
00:01:18,761 --> 00:01:30,121
In twelve eighty-nine, a Mongol ruler wrote to the king of France. His letter named the envoy: Rabban Bar Sauma, a Church of the East monk born in China.

8
00:01:30,771 --> 00:01:43,470
In the early sixteen hundreds, Shah Abbas the First settled Armenians from Julfa at New Julfa, beside Isfahan. They built a famous silk-trade network, and their churches still stand.

9
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:57,970
In eighteen forty, American missionaries at Urmia set a printing press to work, and printed the Lord's Prayer in ancient Syriac. Later they printed whole Testaments.

10
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:14,710
Today, Iran's constitution recognizes Christians as a protected minority, and Armenians hold reserved seats in parliament. But people who convert from Islam are not legally recognized, and estimates of how many Christians live in Iran vary widely.

